## Hard rules

1. **Domain must not import from adapters.** All arrows point inward. Domain
   classes contain zero framework annotations (`@Entity`, `@Autowired`, etc.).
2. **Ports are interfaces.** Input ports (`Get{{pascalCase moduleName}}UseCase`,
   `Create{{pascalCase moduleName}}UseCase`) define what the app CAN do. Output
   ports (`{{pascalCase moduleName}}Repository`) define what the app NEEDS from
   outside.
3. **Adapters implement ports.** The REST controller (`adapter/in`) calls input
   ports. The JPA adapter (`adapter/out`) implements output ports.
4. **JPA entities are adapter-internal.** `{{pascalCase moduleName}}Entity` lives
   in `adapter/out/persistence/` and maps to/from the domain model.
5. **Adding a module means:** domain model + input port + output port + domain
   service + web adapter + persistence adapter.
{{#if useAuth}}
6. **Protected endpoints** require a valid JWT Bearer token.
{{/if}}

## When you are unsure

Prefer asking over guessing. The most common mistake is putting JPA annotations
on domain model classes — those belong in `adapter/out/persistence/` only.
